@prefix :        <https://example.org/chatgpt-models#> .
@prefix choice:  <https://example.org/choice#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

# The words of the page. This domain uses the choice vocabulary (ontology/choice.ttl) directly, so
# it needs no mapping.

<https://example.org/chatgpt-models/domain>
    a choice:Domain ;
    rdfs:label "Which ChatGPT model for this task?"@en ;
    choice:intro "A recommendation for your task, from Alok Sharan's decision tree and the post that goes with it."@en ;
    choice:author "Alok Sharan" ;
    choice:sourceTitle "ChatGPT Model Tree"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://lnkd.in/p/efs4j4NS> ;
    choice:startsAt :FinishedWork ;
    choice:optionsTitle "The six models, from the diagram"@en ;
    choice:ladderTitle "The effort ladder"@en ;
    choice:ladderQuote "Increase capability when the output falls short, rather than defaulting to maximum effort."@en ;
    choice:recordTitle "Model"@en ;
    choice:recordName "a brief"@en ;
    choice:recordFile "brief.md" ;
    choice:recordAppendix """## Your brief

“Start with a clear brief. Share the goal, relevant context, constraints, and what a good result looks like.” (Alok Sharan)

- Goal:
- Relevant context:
- Constraints:
- A good result looks like:

“Keep human review in the workflow, especially when the outcome matters.”"""@en .
